# =============================================================================
# NiftyCasino — production EnvironmentFile (systemd)
#
# Install as /opt/niftycasino/deploy/niftycasino.env, then:
#
#     sudo cp deploy/niftycasino.env.example /opt/niftycasino/deploy/niftycasino.env
#     sudo chmod 600 /opt/niftycasino/deploy/niftycasino.env   # service-role key lives here
#     sudo systemctl restart niftycasino
#
# NOTE: unlike .env, this file is NOT "everything optional". On the VM the app is
# the single scraper and the money path, so DATABASE_URL is required: without it
# the process boots on the in-memory store, takes bets, and loses them all on the
# next restart — silently. Leave a variable commented out ONLY if you can say why.
#
# Never commit the real file. `chmod 600` + owner niftycasino, always.
# =============================================================================

# --- Postgres (game store: bets, wallets, ledger, ticks) ---------------------
# REQUIRED. Project Settings → Database → Connection string (URI).
# Use the SESSION pooler for the app (:5432 on …pooler.supabase.com); keep the
# direct string for migrations/DDL only. See README → "Supabase setup".
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# --- Supabase auth (browser + server session) --------------------------------
# Both from Project Settings → API. Set them together: a URL without the anon key
# disables the dev-auth fallback and leaves auth unusable rather than switching
# modes silently (README → "Auth").
PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...anon...

# --- Supabase auth admin (server only) --------------------------------------
# service_role key: Project Settings → API. BYPASSES ROW LEVEL SECURITY.
# Server-only code reads this; it must never reach a browser.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ...service_role...

# --- HTTP server (adapter-node) ----------------------------------------------
# The unit sets these too; they are repeated here so the env file is the one place
# to change them. HOST must stay on loopback — Caddy is the only front door.
#PORT=3000
#HOST=127.0.0.1
# Seconds the app waits for in-flight requests (and SSE streams) to drain after
# SIGTERM. Must stay smaller than TimeoutStopSec in niftycasino.service.
#SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=30

# --- Upstream feed base URLs (OPTIONAL — the relay path, PLAN §6 R1) ---------
# Leave BOTH commented out unless the Task 3.5 spike showed this VM's datacenter
# IP is blocked. Unset = the real NSE/BSE, exactly as scraped. Set = every NSE/BSE
# request (including the Akamai warm-up) goes to the given origin instead; paths,
# query strings and browser headers are unchanged, and Origin/Referer stay pinned
# to the real sites.
#
#     NSE_BASE_URL=http://<mac>:8081
#     BSE_BASE_URL=http://<mac>:8081
#
# The relay should be reachable over Tailscale/WireGuard, never the public
# internet. docs/RUNBOOK.md → "Blocked-feed triage (R1)" has the decision tree,
# the spike curls to run first, and the relay design.
#NSE_BASE_URL=
#BSE_BASE_URL=

# --- OPS switches (leave unset) ---------------------------------------------
# These are OPERATOR kill switches, not configuration. Default is unset = the
# scheduler runs. Set one to 1 (or true) ONLY for a maintenance window or to stop
# a misbehaving loop, and remember to unset it: a stale kill switch quietly does
# nothing all day and looks exactly like a bug.
#
# CAS_POLLER_DISABLED=1   # stop the 4s NSE/BSE scraper loop (feed blocked, VM maintenance)
# SETTLE_DISABLED=1       # stop the settlement loop 15:43–17:00 IST (rarely wanted)
#
# Neither affects scripts/settle-manual.ts, which is always a deliberate,
# hand-invoked action — that is the point.
